{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Is it for HaShem that you requite this?</b> That you bring ruin upon your Father who is in heaven, when He has done for you many kindnesses, as he proceeds to recount. It further appears to me to interpret the verse הצור ('the Rock'): since it was the day of his death, he was justifying the judgment upon himself and saying that all His ways are justice — all the ways of the Blessed One, and even death contains in them nothing crooked or perverse; אל אמונה ('a God of faithfulness'), that is, since death in this world is a judgment of faithfulness, meaning: He is faithful to pay him reward in the world of souls for the righteous deeds he performed, in the manner of what is written (I Samuel 26:23): ״וה׳ ישיב לאיש את צדקתו ואת אמונתו״, and it is written (Psalms 89): ״ועד בשחק נאמן סלה״. ואין עול ('and without injustice'), that is, He is faithful also to exact payment to punish him for his sin — this is what it says ואין עול, meaning, even if He punishes him in the world of souls, there is no injustice in His judgment. צדיק וישר הוא ('righteous and upright is He') — righteous in the world of resurrection, and upright for the World to Come that follows resurrection. And he alludes to us here four worlds: this world, the world of souls, the world of resurrection, and the World to Come that follows it. And you already know that they are alluded to in a single verse, namely what is written (Proverbs 6:22): בהתהלכך תנחה אותך בשכבך תשמור עליך והקיצות היא תשיחך — 'בהתהלכך תנחה אותך' in this world; 'בשכבך תשמור עליך' for the world of souls; 'והקיצות' for the resurrection of the dead; 'היא תשיחך' for the World to Come; thus did the Rabbis of blessed memory expound it. Also here in the verse הצור these four worlds are alluded to, for which the soul of the righteous is destined. And according to this interpretation, how pleasant and fitting is the custom in this passage that is fixed for rendering judgment on the deceased to say it over the dead — for thus said the master of all the prophets on the day of his death, and he justified the judgment upon himself. And it is further possible to say that he justified the judgment upon himself in the language of צור ('Rock') because he sinned through it and provoked it, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 17:6): הנני עומד לפניך שם על הצור. And it was not Moses' way throughout the entire Torah to invoke names of might and lordship, but only the name of mercy alone — except in two places in the Torah where he invoked יה: (Exodus 15) עזי וזמרת יה, and (Exodus 17) כי יד על כס יה. But now he invoked the language of צור because he wished to praise Him through that in which he sinned — in the manner of what the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: Moses said, באז חטאתי (Exodus 5) and מאז באתי אל פרעה — through באז I praise Him, as it says (Exodus 15) אז ישיר משה." "<b>And if</b> you understand further in this verse, you will find in it an allusion to the seven sefirot of wisdom: תמים פעלו ('His work is perfect') — this is the attribute of חסד; כי כל דרכיו משפט ('for all His ways are justice') — this is the attribute of גבורה; אל אמונה ('a God of faithfulness') — this is אמת, which is the middle line; ואין עול ('and without injustice') — this is נצח, as it is said (I Samuel 15:29): ״וגם נצח ישראל לא ישקר ולא ינחם״; צדיק ('righteous') — this is יסוד עולם; וישר ('and upright') — this is the מלכות. And it says הוא ('He') in order to bind and unify everything as one without separation." "<b>They have dealt corruptly with Him, not His children — their blemish.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: שחת לו לעצמו — they corrupted themselves, for a מום (blemish) is called שחתה (corruption), in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 22:25): ״כי משחתם בהם מום בם״. And He called them לא בניו ('not His children'), just as He had called them בניו ('His children') in the time of favor; and since the Father is righteous and upright while the children are the opposite, for they are דור עקש ופתלתל ('a twisted and perverse generation'), therefore לא בניו. And this is correct. However, since the word לא carries the sense of refusal (טרחא), and the word בניו is attached to מומם, it is therefore possible to interpret: שחת לו לא — that what Israel corrupted for itself is in their defiance of the word of the Blessed One, in the manner of (Isaiah 42:24): ״ולא אבו בדרכיו הלוך״ — for all that Moses would lead them and guide them on the straight path, they would say לא נעשה ('we will not do it'), and this is שחת לו לא — Israel corrupted itself with the word לא. And since He called them בניו (Deuteronomy 14:1): בנים אתם לה׳ אלהיכם — behold, this rebelliousness and corruption is a great blemish upon them, that they should be דור עקש ופתלתל." "<b>Is it for HaShem that you requite this, O foolish nation?</b> That the לאו ('no') itself was at the receiving of the Torah; and thus Targum renders (Exodus 24): נבול תבול מלאה תלאי. And this is the view of Onkelos, who said: עמא דקבילו אוריתא ולא חכימו — a nation that received the Torah and did not grow wise. If so, the expression is not derogatory but rather an added virtue." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote concerning the word נבל that it refers to a man who denies the kindness his fellow did for him; and he said that one who does good freely is called נדיב, and one who repays evil to one who did good to him is called נבל. And thus it is written (Isaiah 32): לא יקרא עוד לנבל נדיב — for he is its opposite. Therefore Scripture said of Nabal the Carmelite (I Samuel 25): כשמו כן הוא נבל שמו ונבלה עמו — for David did him a great kindness and guarded all that was his, but he refused to repay him recompense and disdained him and his messengers. And this is the meaning of the verse regarding Job's companions (Job 42): לבלתי עשות עמכם נבלה — for they were arguing in defense of the deeds of the Blessed One, that they are just, and if He were to harm them, נבלה would appear — thus far." "<b>And He said: Is He not your Father, who has acquired you.</b> That is to say that He called you a possession, for He brought you forth from nothingness to being, and all being is a possession; and this is what is said (Proverbs 8:22): ״ה' קנני ראשית דרכו״, and likewise (Genesis 14:19): ״קונה שמים וארץ״ — that is, He created heaven and earth and brought them forth from nothingness to being. But in Bereshit Rabbah they interpreted the word קונה according to its plain sense, that He acquired it from another, and that is the primordial matter; and I have already mentioned this in the section of לך לך and brought that midrash there. And the Holy One, blessed be He, is called our Father in relation to the soul, for He begot it and breathed it into our nostrils, just as the one who begets is called father in relation to the body that he begot; and He, exalted be He, is the primal, enduring, and eternal Father. And so David, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 27:10): ״כי אבי ואמי עזבוני וה' יאספני״, and likewise Isaiah said (Isaiah 63:16): ״כי אתה אבינו כי אברהם לא ידענו וישראל לא יכירנו אתה ה' אבינו גואלנו מעולם שמך״." "<b>And know</b> that in the word הלה', the letter ה is written alone, and it is vocalized with a pataḥ, and under the ל is a silent shva. And it should have been that the ל receive a shva na, as in ליעקב, but it is impossible for a person to pronounce two shva sounds one after the other, and therefore we were required to leave the shva under the ל silent, so that the shva of the י might be vocal (na) and the vowel-point of the Name not be hidden. And behold, this word is unique — there is none like it in all of Scripture. And likewise it is impossible for the ל to be vocalized with a ḥirik, as in ליהודה, for then the vowel-point of the י of the Name would also be hidden, just as it is hidden in the name יהודה, and therefore it was necessary that the word be pointed as it is." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to understand further, that this letter ה, written alone, is a large ה, among the twenty-two large letters that exist in Scripture." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah.</b> It is possible to say that it alludes to the first ה in the Name. And open the eye of your reason and you will see how Moses our teacher, of blessed memory, mentioned a small ה at the beginning of the Torah and a large ה at the end of the Torah, to allude by this that the small ה created by means of the power of the large ה, where the return of the souls is found, and concerning this Moses alluded thus on the day of his death. And it is known to every discerning one that when Israel are worthy they add strength and abundance to the Shekhinah, and when they are guilty that strength is cut off, and then the Shekhinah refuses to be comforted over her children, for that abundance is not present; and therefore it says: הלה' תגמלו זאת — will you sever the quality called זאת from the first ה in the Name, so that it not receive abundance from it; and from this it says of the wise: ישכילו זאת — that is, they draw down to the quality of זאת from the source of wisdom and understanding; and this is יבינו לאחריתם — they draw down understanding to the end and do not separate it. הלא הוא אביך קנך — is the first ה, which is the nest (קן) of the world, your Father; He acquired you (קנך) in the mystery of the commandment of sending away the nest (שלוח הקן); and the term קן is the first foundation in the coming-into-being of birds. וה\"א אחרונה עשך — and the last ה made you, in the language of (Psalms 100:3): ״הוא עשנו ולא אנחנו״. And the word ויכוננך: He prepared you to receive the repair." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ויכוננך — the roll that contains everything: from Him are His priests, from Him are His prophets, from Him are His sages, from Him are His scribes; and likewise it says (Zechariah 10:4): ״ממנו פנה ממנו יתד״. For Pharaoh found among all the sages of his kingdom no one to interpret his dream but Joseph, and likewise Nebuchadnezzar found among all the sages of Babylon none but Daniel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :register {:flags {0 ["God"], 1 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The prooftexts he strings here are cited by their opening words; I have retained the Hebrew quotation forms and normalized the parenthetical citations.", 2 "The word טרחא is an Aramaic term for refusal/obstinacy; left untranslated as it functions here as a technical grammatical note."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:45.100608537Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}